  "codebook": {
    "definition": "Credit-claiming occurs when a senator explicitly attributes a positive outcome, action, or policy to their own efforts, advocacy, or legislative work, thereby seeking recognition or responsibility for the result.",
    "clarification": "Credit-claiming is identified when a senator uses first-person language (e.g., 'I secured,' 'I helped,' 'I introduced') or explicitly links their actions to a tangible benefit, funding, or policy change. It often involves highlighting past or ongoing efforts that directly led to a favorable result for their constituents, state, or a specific group. The focus is on the senator taking personal or legislative ownership of the outcome.",
    "positive_examples": [
